# Can any voting rule be fair? Arrow's impossibility theorem
subtitle: "A town that votes in circles, the theorem that says no voting rule is perfect, and why that does not make democracy impossible."
tags: ["economics","political science","social choice","voting","arrow impossibility theorem","democracy"]
teaches: ["concepts/arrow-impossibility-theorem","concepts/condorcet-paradox","concepts/single-peaked-preferences","concepts/agenda-setting"]
thumb: "band \"No fair voting system exists. Or does it?\" stamp \"PARADOX\""
voices: {"a":"gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed","b":"gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace","take":"lines"}
prompt: "Why can a majority prefer A to B, B to C and C to A? Why must every fair-looking voting rule break one reasonable condition?"

## Arrow's impossibility theorem
sources:
    - id: arrow1951
      title: Social Choice and Individual Values
      authors: Kenneth J. Arrow
      year: 1951
      finding: With three or more options, no rule turning individual rankings into a group ranking satisfies unrestricted domain, Pareto (unanimity), independence of irrelevant alternatives and non-dictatorship (the Pareto form is from the 1963 second edition). Arrow shared the 1972 Nobel prize in economics with John Hicks.
    - id: black1948
      title: On the Rationale of Group Decision-making
      authors: Duncan Black
      year: 1948
      finding: 'If preferences are single-peaked along one dimension, majority rule has a winner: the median voter''s favourite (Journal of Political Economy 56: 23–34).'
    - id: riker1982
      title: Liberalism Against Populism
      authors: William H. Riker
      year: 1982
      finding: Social-choice results make the 'will of the people' (populism) meaningless; voting can only serve the liberal aim of removing officials.
    - id: mackie2003
      title: Democracy Defended
      authors: Gerry Mackie
      year: 2003
      finding: Re-examines Riker's and other published claims of majority cycles and finds most do not hold up; cycling and manipulation are not frequent or harmful enough to undermine democracy.
    - id: regenwetter2006
      title: 'Behavioral Social Choice: Probabilistic Models, Statistical Inference, and Applications'
      authors: Michel Regenwetter, Bernard Grofman, A. A. J. Marley and Ilia Tsetlin
      year: 2006
      finding: Worries about the prevalence of majority cycles are greatly overstated; virtually no empirical evidence of the Condorcet paradox in survey and ballot data.
    - id: tsetlin2003
      title: The impartial culture maximizes the probability of majority cycles
      authors: Ilia Tsetlin, Michel Regenwetter and Bernard Grofman
      year: 2003
      finding: 'Under the impartial culture (random rankings) the chance of a cycle with three options in a large electorate is about 8.8 %; any departure from it lowers that chance in large electorates (Social Choice and Welfare 21: 387–398). Our own exact count gives 12 of 216 (5.6 %) for three voters, and simulation about 25 % for five options in a large electorate.'
      doi: 10.1007/s00355-003-0269-z
    - id: mckelvey1976
      title: Intransitivities in multidimensional voting models and some implications for agenda control
      authors: Richard D. McKelvey
      year: 1976
      finding: 'With two or more issue dimensions, majority rule generally has no stable point; an agenda setter can lead a sequence of majority votes to almost any outcome (Journal of Economic Theory 12: 472–482).'
    - id: rikerbrams1973
      title: The Paradox of Vote Trading
      authors: William H. Riker and Steven J. Brams
      year: 1973
      finding: 'When every legislator trades votes, each trade pays for the traders yet everyone can end up worse off (American Political Science Review 67: 1235–1247); Bernholz (1973) tied vote trading to cyclical majorities.'

