    card title "Is there a fair way to vote?"

A: Here's a question that sounds simple: when people disagree, is there a fair way to turn everyone's rankings into one choice for the group?
    draw th1 th2 th3 t_rule tr1 tr2 tr3

B: Easy. Majority rule. Whatever most people prefer, the group prefers.
    draw cue_major

    erase cue_major

A: Let's test that. A town of three equal groups must build one thing: a pool, a park or a library.
    draw n_pool n_park n_lib

A: Group one ranks pool, then park, then library. (@draw col2@) Group two: park, library, pool. (@draw col3@) Group three: library, pool, park.
    draw col1

A: Now vote in pairs. Pool against park: groups (@draw a_pp l_pp@) one and three put the pool higher, so it wins two to one.

    ask "Park against library: which wins? Tap it." choose ["n_park","n_lib"] answer n_park store pk voice b
        right: The park, two to one.

A: The park, two to one: groups one and two both rank it above the library.
    draw a_pl l_pl

    erase pk_ring

B: So the pool beats the park, (@highlight a_pl l_pl duration 1.8@) and the park beats the library. (@draw a_guess l_guess@) Then the pool must beat the library.
    highlight a_pp l_pp duration 1.8

    erase a_guess l_guess

A: That's what logic says. But groups two and (@draw a_lp l_lp@) three both prefer the library, so it beats the pool, two to one.

    erase table

A: The marquis de Condorcet spotted this in 1785: every voter is perfectly consistent, and yet the majority (@flow a_pp a_pl a_lp duration 2.5@) goes round in a circle.
    draw condorcet

    erase condorcet

A: That circle is our thread: who it hands power to, why Kenneth Arrow proved no voting rule fully escapes it, and how much it should worry us.
    blocking false

    flow a_pp a_pl a_lp duration 4

    highlight tri effect box color #8f887c duration 3.5

B: Round and round, then. So which one does the town actually build?
    flow a_pp a_pl a_lp

A: That depends on the order of the votes. Say the chair puts pool against park first: the pool wins, then meets the library, and loses.
    draw v1 b1_r1 v2 b1_r2

    ask "Now you chair the meeting, and you want the park. Which option do you hold back for the final vote? Tap it." choose ["n_pool","n_park","n_lib"] answer n_park stage own store chair voice b
        right: Hold back the park: the library wins the first vote, and the park beats the library.
        wrong: Whatever you hold back meets the winner of the other two. Which option beats that winner?

    erase b1_r1 b1_r2 chair_ring

A: Hold the park back. Pool against library first, the library wins, and then the park beats the library. Whatever comes in last wins.
    draw b2_r1 b2_r2 last_in

B: So whoever sets the order picks the winner, and every single vote along the way looks fair.
    draw box_b2

    clear

    card title "Arrow's theorem"

A: Could a cleverer rule fix it? In 1951 a young economist, Kenneth Arrow, asked whether any rule at all could do the job fairly.
    draw arrow_p

    erase arrow_p

A: He asked for very little. Any rankings are allowed. If everyone prefers pool to park, so does the group. And no dictator, whose ranking always wins.
    draw k_any k_una k_dict

A: The result must be a proper ranking, with no circles. And the verdict on pool against park can't depend on where people put the library.
    draw k_circ k_ind

B: That's the bare minimum. Any decent rule would pass.
    highlight k_any k_una k_dict k_circ k_ind effect underline color #2f6b8f

A: Arrow proved that with three or more options, no rule meets all five at once. It's part of why he shared the 1972 Nobel prize.
    draw box_all never

    erase k_any k_una k_dict k_circ k_ind box_all never

B: What about points, then? Two for first place, one for second. No circles there.
    draw borda

A: No circles, but take five voters: three rank pool, park, library; two rank park, library, pool. On points the park wins, seven to six.
    draw bd_cols bd_tally bd_box_park

A: Now the library drops out. Nobody changes their mind about pool and park, yet the pool wins, three to two. Points give up independence.
    draw bd_x1 bd_x2 bd_x3 bd_res bd_ind

    highlight bd_ind effect box color #2f6b8f duration 2

    clear

    card title "Is democracy doomed?"

B: So that's it: Arrow proved democracy is impossible.
    draw q_dem

A: That's the strong reading. In 1982 William Riker argued that votes can't reveal a will of the people; at best they let us throw rulers out.
    draw riker_n riker_q

    erase riker_n riker_q q_dem

A: But the theorem is narrower: no rule works for every possible pattern of rankings. It says nothing about how often the bad patterns turn up.
    draw o_base o_title

A: Take the gloomiest model: every voter picks a ranking at random, like rolling a die.
    draw dice

A: With three voters and three options, a circle turns up 12 times in 216, about 6 percent.
    draw o1 o1_note

    ask "And with millions of voters, still three options? Set your guess." estimate.min 0 estimate.max 30 estimate.value 8.8 estimate.unit % store g stage own default "15" voice b
        right: Close: you said {g}, and it's {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; it's {g.true}.

    erase dice

A: Just under 9 percent. And with five options instead of three, about one in four.
    draw o2 o3

A: But real voters aren't dice. Their views share patterns, and Michel Regenwetter and colleagues showed that pushes circles down; in surveys and ballots they found almost none.
    draw o4 o4_src

A: And when Gerry Mackie rechecked the famous cases of cycling in 2003, Riker's among them, most of them didn't hold up.
    draw mackie mk_x

    clear

    card title "Ways out"

B: So what keeps real electorates out of the circle?
    draw ax ax_y xs xm xl

A: Often, the options line up on one scale. (@draw pk1@) Say a small, (@draw pk2@) medium or (@draw pk3@) large budget, and (@highlight pk1 pk2 pk3 duration 2.5@) each voter likes options less the further they are from a favourite.

A: That (@highlight pk2 duration 2.5@) shape is the rescue. Duncan Black showed in 1948 that then there's no circle: (@draw pt_med lab_med@) the voter in the middle, the median voter, is on the winning side of every pair.

B: And our town failed that test. Every option was someone's last choice, so no order of the three gives everyone a single peak.
    draw town

    erase ax ax_y xs xm xl peaks pt_med lab_med town

A: Other rules step outside rankings altogether. In score or approval voting you grade each option, so Arrow's theorem doesn't apply; but grades must mean the same to everyone, and tactical voting still pays.
    draw grades gr_cost

    erase grades gr_cost

B: And politicians trade votes: you back my bridge, I'll back your hospital.
    draw trade lr_s1 lr_s2 lr_swap

A: Log-rolling lets strong feelings count, which rankings can't. But it's no cure: vote trading is (@draw lr_d1 lr_d2 lr_all@) tied to circles too, and when everyone trades, everyone can end up worse off.

    clear

    card title "Where it bites"

B: I'd have guessed small committees are where circles really lurk.
    draw o_base o_title o1

A: On pure chance, no: three voters circle a little less often than millions. But in a big electorate shared views average out and circles fade; a small committee gets no such protection.
    draw o2 o4

    erase o_base o_title o1 o2 o4

A: And committees weigh issues with many sides at once. Richard McKelvey showed in 1976 that then majority rule can circle almost everywhere, so whoever sets the agenda can steer the vote nearly anywhere.
    draw pl_ax pl_x pl_y members ag_start ag1 wp1 ag2 wp2 ag3 wp3 ag4 wp4 ag5 ag_end ag_lab

    clear

    card title "Is there a fair way to vote?"

A: So Arrow doesn't say democracy is impossible. He says no ranking rule is perfect: each gives up one of these, or steps outside rankings altogether.
    draw conds gives

B: So choose what to give up, on purpose, and keep an eye on whoever sets the order.
    highlight gives effect underline color #2f6b8f

    quiz "A five-member board picks one of four sites by votes in pairs, and the chair sets the order. What should worry you most?" voice a
        right: The order: if the sites form a circle, the one brought in last wins, and with four options a circle is more likely than with three.
        wrong: Think back to the pool, the park and the library: what changed when the park was held back?
        * Nothing: each vote is a fair majority
        + The order may decide the winner
        * Arrow says it can't decide at all

    erase conds gives

    move tri to.x 500 to.y 440 duration 0.2

B: So, a fair way to add up our rankings? Not a perfect one. But a good one, if we know what we gave up.
    draw tri

    flow a_pp a_pl a_lp duration 2.5

