audio:
  lang: en
  lines:
    '|a||Here''s a question that sounds simple: when people disagree, is there a fair way to turn everyone''s rankings into one choice for the group?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8280
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|b||Easy. Majority rule. Whatever most people prefer, the group prefers.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4360
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:cu9upm'
    '|a||Let''s test that. A town of three equal groups must build one thing: a pool, a park or a library.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7160
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1pnkam4'
    '|a||Group one ranks pool, then park, then library. Group two: park, library, pool. Group three: library, pool, park.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7760
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1gu7dhs'
    '|a||Now vote in pairs. Pool against park: groups one and three put the pool higher, so it wins two to one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7280
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1kec55f'
    '|b||Park against library: which wins? Tap it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4080
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:shn6jk'
    '|b||The park, two to one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 1640
      voice: gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace
    '|a||The park, two to one: groups one and two both rank it above the library.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4880
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|b||So the pool beats the park, and the park beats the library. Then the pool must beat the library.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5760
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1kusdc8'
    '|a||That''s what logic says. But groups two and three both prefer the library, so it beats the pool, two to one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7080
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:14p6ppr'
    '|a||The marquis de Condorcet spotted this in 1785: every voter is perfectly consistent, and yet the majority goes round in a circle.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8800
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:ylv1ei'
    '|a||That circle is our thread: who it hands power to, why Kenneth Arrow proved no voting rule fully escapes it, and how much it should worry us.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8800
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:14z55zw'
    '|b||Round and round, then. So which one does the town actually build?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3840
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:tz8ld4'
    '|a||That depends on the order of the votes. Say the chair puts pool against park first: the pool wins, then meets the library, and loses.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8800
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:whdvql'
    '|b||Now you chair the meeting, and you want the park. Which option do you hold back for the final vote? Tap it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6200
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1iwgkct'
    '|b||Hold back the park: the library wins the first vote, and the park beats the library.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5680
      voice: gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace
    '|a||Hold the park back. Pool against library first, the library wins, and then the park beats the library. Whatever comes in last wins.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8160
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|b||So whoever sets the order picks the winner, and every single vote along the way looks fair.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5880
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:awhrlp'
    '|a||Could a cleverer rule fix it? In 1951 a young economist, Kenneth Arrow, asked whether any rule at all could do the job fairly.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8920
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1m6tp75'
    '|a||He asked for very little. Any rankings are allowed. If everyone prefers pool to park, so does the group. And no dictator, whose ranking always wins.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9640
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:av51ty'
    '|a||The result must be a proper ranking, with no circles. And the verdict on pool against park can''t depend on where people put the library.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8120
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1dzkyh6'
    '|b||That''s the bare minimum. Any decent rule would pass.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3240
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:io690h'
    '|a||Arrow proved that with three or more options, no rule meets all five at once. It''s part of why he shared the 1972 Nobel prize.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7840
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:oyo47'
    '|b||What about points, then? Two for first place, one for second. No circles there.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4920
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:4euyl9'
    '|a||No circles, but take five voters: three rank pool, park, library; two rank park, library, pool. On points the park wins, seven to six.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11480
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:oziaf3'
    '|a||Now the library drops out. Nobody changes their mind about pool and park, yet the pool wins, three to two. Points give up independence.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8920
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm economist who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1x96b8k'
    '|b||So that''s it: Arrow proved democracy is impossible.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3080
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:ymb6zo'
    '|a||That''s the strong reading. In 1982 William Riker argued that votes can''t reveal a will of the people; at best they let us throw rulers out.':
